  "answer_text": "In Massachusetts, the maximum amount you can collect for unemployment depends on your weekly benefit rate, whether you have dependents, and the number of weeks you are eligible to receive benefits.\n\nThe maximum amounts are as follows:\n\n### 1. Maximum Weekly Benefit Amount\n* **Standard Maximum:** **$1,105 per week**. *(This rate became effective on October 5, 2025, and remains active through October 2026).*\n* **With Dependents:** If you have dependent children, you can receive an additional **$25 per week per dependent**. This dependency allowance is capped at **50% of your weekly benefit amount** (meaning an eligible claimant receiving the maximum $1,105 rate could technically receive up to an additional $552.50 per week if they have enough qualifying dependents).\n\n### 2. Maximum Duration of Benefits\n* **Maximum Weeks:** You can currently collect benefits for up to **30 weeks**. \n* *Note:* While the standard limit is usually 26 weeks, Massachusetts law automatically extends this to 30 weeks if the 12-month average unemployment rate in any of the state's metropolitan areas exceeds 5.1%. \n\n### 3. Maximum Total Benefit (Over the Full Claim Period)\n* **Without Dependents:** If you qualify for the maximum weekly rate ($1,105) for the full 30 weeks, the total maximum you can collect is **$33,150**.\n* **With Dependents:** If you receive the dependency allowance, your total maximum layout will be higher depending on the number of dependent children you claim.",
